questions.question_text:
This solution to Longest Consecutive Sequence contains a nested loop. Why is it O(n) rather than O(n^2)?

```python
num_set = set(nums)
for num in num_set:
    if num - 1 not in num_set:
        length = 1
        while num + length in num_set:
            length += 1
```

questions.correct_answer:
**General binary tree**: you have no ordering information, so you must search. Do a postorder DFS that returns the LCA if found in a subtree, otherwise the node itself if it matches p or q, otherwise None. If both children return non-None, the current node is the split point and therefore the LCA. O(n) time, O(h) space for the call stack.

**BST**: the ordering invariant lets you walk straight down without searching. Starting at the root, if both target values are less than the current node, go left; if both are greater, go right; otherwise the values split here (or one of them IS the current node), so this is the LCA. O(h) time and O(1) space iteratively.

```python
while root:
    if p.val < root.val and q.val < root.val:
        root = root.left
    elif p.val > root.val and q.val > root.val:
        root = root.right
    else:
        return root
```

The difference — O(n) and O(h) space versus O(h) and O(1) space — is a concrete illustration of what the BST invariant buys you. Using the general algorithm on a BST is correct but leaves that value on the table.

